    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d737">

                <pb facs="0862" n="850" xml:id="pg850"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">390.1115/2891: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Harrison</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-04-23">April 23, 1942</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1029. American interests—Japan, Philippine welfare inquiries. Your 1508,
                        14th.<anchor n="95" xml:id="d737-f1"/> Department greatly appreciates action
                    Swiss Minister at Tokyo in obtaining information regarding Americans in
                    Manila.</p>
                <p>Department will hold individual Philippine inquiries to minimum and continue to
                    refer inquiries generally to Red Cross. However, Department considers Japanese
                    Government obligated by its engagement to apply the Prisoners of War
                        Convention<anchor n="96" xml:id="d737-f2"/> to civilian internees to furnish
                    this Government with information concerning the welfare of such internees.
                    Report is urgently desired in regard to conditions under which Americans are
                    interned at Manila, and in particular in regard to welfare of American
                    Government personnel at Manila.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d737-f1" n="95" xml:id="d737-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported that messages on Americans in Manila had been received
                    by the Swiss Legation at Tokyo from the Japanese Foreign Office and that
                    information regarding Americans in other occupied areas of the Philippines might
                    eventually be obtained through the same channels if inquiries were not
                    numerous.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d737-f2" n="96" xml:id="d737-f2-content">
                <p>Signed at Geneva, July 27, 1929, <ref target="frus1929v01#pg_336"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1929, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 336</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
